# KNOWN GAP (`todo/t1068`) — OVER-REJECTION. The one cell of the closure-escape
# 2×2 that the escape diagnostic actually inspects is now rejected for a hazard
# that no longer exists.
#
#   | capture root | spelling               | today                              |
#   |--------------|------------------------|------------------------------------|
#   | param        | `return (): …` literal | runs, correct                      |
#   | param        | `f = …; return f`      | runs, correct                      |
#   | local        | `return (): …` literal | runs, correct                      |
#   | local        | `f = …; return f`      | ⛔ `error[E_ClosureEscapesScope]`   |
#
# `check_expr_for_escaping_closures` (`src/semantic/safety/helpers.rs`) matches
# the returned expression against `Expr::Identifier` / `Expr::StructLiteral` /
# `Expr::ArrayLiteral`. There is NO `Expr::Closure` arm, so a returned closure
# LITERAL is never inspected at all — the diagnostic keys on `local AND named`,
# which is a SPELLING, not a hazard. The three unrejected cells were the unsound
# ones; the rejected one is the same program written with a name.
#
# The premise the message states — "captures local variable `p` which will be
# dropped" — is no longer true. A by-value capture is materialised at the
# capture site, so the environment holds an independent value and the local's
# scope exit frees only the local's own buffer. The three sibling cells prove it
# by running correctly:
# `closure_escape_capture_axis_local_literal.gg` is THIS PROGRAM with the
# binding inlined.
#
# ⚠ DO NOT "fix" this by widening the diagnostic to the closure-literal arm —
# that would reject three correct programs instead of accepting one. The
# rescue owed is retiring the over-rejection (D34 territory), and the
# discriminator to delete is `local AND named`.
#
# INTENDED (asserted by the `#[ignore]`d
# `known_gap_closure_escape_local_named_capture_over_rejected`):
# prints `hello!`, rc 0, both backends, ASan-clean.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

Callable[String()] make_local():
    String p = mk("hel", "lo")
    Callable[String()] f = (): mk(p, "!")
    return f

void main():
    Callable[String()] g = make_local()
    print(g())
